a 20 million dollar effort to rid the  ocean of nearly two trillion pieces of  plastic is on hold after a major  breakdown the group behind the ocean  cleanup project says part of its 2,000  foot boom broke off and the system is  now being towed to Hawaii  the boom was trying to collect a massive  area of floating trash in the Pacific  that's twice the size of Texas it's  known as the Great Pacific Garbage Patch  Carter Evans spoke with the young  inventor behind the ambitious plan  nearly four months after setting sail  from San Francisco it appears the ocean  may have been too much for the device  designed to clean it 24-year old Dutch  inventor Boyan slat says the system  consisting of a massive pipe and  underwater skirt is now being towed back  to port after the crew discovered a  nearly 60 foot section of the boom broke  off what we saw last week is what it's  likely caused by what's called fatigue  our system has been moving up and down  in big waves around one-and-a-half  million times and that's what's likely  cost this fraction it's designed for 20  years we first met slide in September  just before the launch are you sure it  will work  nope we'll see in the coming months  right the device was designed to use the  wind waves and water currents to skim  plastic off the surface and collect the  trash but by mid-december project  leaders noticed the system would  sometimes travel slower than the plastic  allowing the trash to float back into  the ocean slat says it managed to  collect about 4,500 pounds of plastic in  its initial run far from his goal of  about 2,200 pounds per week my hope now  is that after having several months of  experience and two failures that they  would step back and see is there a  better use for this device can we modify  the design and use it to capture  plastics before they enter the ocean  despite these setbacks do you think this  is still a viable system first of all I  think we are really quite close to to  making it work I don't think  have much choice this plastic doesn't go  away by itself slat says after repairs  the system should be back  skimming trash from the great Garbage  Patch later this year for CBS this  morning Carter Evans Los Angeles let's  hope that works yes I think everybody  would like to see it work
 

